Changes Log for Caliban v2.0

1997 Apr 14: [rwp]

   Modified InitDiskTable.c to make it a little more sophisticated vis-a-vis
   errors on trying to validate spool devices.

   Modified Caliban.c as follows: 

1) Call to InitDiskTables now just looks at the number of valid devices
   detected, now kept out of the disktab structure, but in the new numvalid
   variable.  
   If numvalid=0, prints an error 
   If numvalid<disktab->numdisks, prints a warning to the effect that the
   Caliban.ini file contains invalid spool devices

2) CHKSTATUS command, now prints the status of all spool devices in the
   Caliban.ini file.  As before, it would only print the first (0:numvalid) as
   it did not check the "valid" flag on the loop.  This was confusing, as we
   had the situation that 4 spool devs were in the .ini file, but only 2 were
   valid.  If the first 2 spool devices in Caliban.ini were valid, they would
   get printed, but if the first two were invalid, they were not printed, as
   the printout only looped 0:1 instead of 0:3.  We need to know if a disk
   came up invalid on startup, so we now print the whole table

1997 Apr 24: [rwp]

   Modified GetFITS.c to remove the .fits extension for now.  Will adopt
   a more sensible strategy later.


1997 Apr 29: [rwp]

   Modified GetFITS.c to use ICIMACS/Prospero conformal keyword=value pairs
   in STATUS: messages (so they can be parsed into internal tables in
   Prospero & friends).  Hook in TransferDisk.c to make Caliban "shutup" has
   been protected by #ifdef SHUTUP.


1997 May 3: [brh]

   Modified GetFITS.c to pad data segment with zeroes instead of spaces.
   Added support for LASTFILE and PATH serial port commands.
   Added more descriptive error messages using errno.h facilities

1997 May 8: [brh]

   Added support for VERSION serial port command

1997 May 19: [rwp]

   Added ADDFITS and DOARCHIVE #defs to Caliban.h

   ADDFITS will append .fits to filenames. Normally #undef at OSU, enabled at
   KPNO to support the [broken] IRAF beta-test FITS kernel that requires a
   .fits extension.

   DOARCHIVE will enable data archiving via the ARCHIVE_CMD command.
   Normally #undef at OSU, only enabled at KPNO where the STB archiver is
   used.

1997 May 27: [brh]

   Improved logging functionality to include all console messages
   Added "Initiating file transfer iteration #n..." console message

1997 September 4: [brh]

   Fixed problem resulting from embedded nulls in FITS filename and uniquename
   header cards.  Previously, Caliban assumed the last character of every card
   value was a quote.  If the filename was less than 8 characters and the
   filename ended with a null, Caliban was blindly chopping the last
   character, thinking it was a null.  Now it first checks to make sure the
   last character is a quote before chopping.

   Fixed problem with null padding on FITS data segment.  Pad buffer contained
   garbage character in first byte since pad constructor loop control variable
   initialized to 1 instead of 0.

1997 October 2: [brh]

   Fixed problem with FITS segment padding routine where if header or data
   segment worked out to an exact multiple of 2880, a pad of 2880 was added.

   Added STATUS: MACHINETYPE=DOWNSTREAM reply to PONG.

   Fixed problem where spurious carriage return (\r) characters appearing on
   the end of messages sent to XmitMsg routine would cause the message to be
   displayed and then immediately overwritten on the screen or when viewing
   the log file, thus making it appear as though the messages were never
   there.

   Added FITS_DATA_BLOCK_SIZE constant to allow changing block size for FITS
   data segment transfers.

   Changed the way FITS header segment is read so that once the header size is
   discovered, a buffer is allocated such that the header segment can be
   read/written in one chunk.

1998 Jan 12: [rwp]

    Modified GetFITS.c to make the error messages more descriptive if the
    SIMPLE or END cards are invalid (as it stands, the error message is
    somewhat misleading).

1998 Jan 22: [brh]

    Added doarchive, dodisplay, doautolog, and addfits parameters to system
    table, ini file, and keyboard/serial command list (previously used #DEFs).

    Added restore command to reset these new system variables to their default
    values as in the ini file.

    Added check for available disk space prior to transferring file.

    Changed failover mechanism for filenames and uniquenames such that any
    problem with a filename will fail over to the unique name (i.e. 
    file already exists or could not create that file name for any
    reason).  Subsequent failure to create unique name causes file to be
    skipped and reported.

1998 Mar 19: [brh]

    Added recognition of "STATUS: MachineType=" messages supporting
    DOWNSTREAM, UPSTREAM, SWITCHED, and PONG keywords.

    Changed it so that only the serialhost can initiate an INIT DISK
    with Caliban.

1998 May 5: [rwp]

    Ported to Solaris 2.6.  Sucked in the usual way ports to SysV sucked.
    Among the features of this voodoo port:

        1) sys_errlist[] is not defined as before, but strerror(errno) works
           just fine.  Did some ugly #def hacking in Caliban.h to make an
           ERRSTRING flag which is one or the other if we can use
           sys_errlist[].

        2) Added more verbose error messages if InitDiskTable() encounters
           either an invalid disk device or cannot read the word DISK out of
           the first line of that disk.  Now says why it cannot get a valid
           disk and what grossed it out, rather than a blind statement about
           an invalid spool device, which carried essentially no information
           content (you gotta say which spool device and why!!)

        3) This one is totally weird with the proverbial beard.  For some
           reason, in the Caliban.c main keystroke parser, it could not do
           "case '\n':" correctly.  I finally resorted to "case 14:" and it
           worked.  Something implementation dependent about fgetc() in the
           solaris edition of gcc?  Dunno.  Weird.

    In other fun:

    Formatting the disks under Solaris was no fun.  The format command behaves
    differently.  You have to make a new kind of disk with the
    ``DISK#.BUS#.mm/dd/yy.'' string, and remember to label it, too, the name
    of the partition is not the name of the disk.  And don't forget to do a
    save, kids!
  
    On kepler (the Solaris 2.6 box going to CTIO), the disks were scsi targets
    0 and 1 on the second controller, and we partitioned the whole shebang as
    ``2'' (that's ``c'' to all you in bsd/sunos land).  The full ugly device
    name is /dev/dsk/c1t0d0s2, which is kindly aliased to /dev/sd15c and
    similarly for /dev/sd16c for disk on target 1 (that weird name syntax
    actually has info content: controller#target#disk#segment#, I think).

    Added a prototype autologging hook right after the archiving stuff
    in GetFITS.c and Caliban.h.  This is commented out right now, but
    we're close to a prototype autologger script.  Stay tuned...

